1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,500
Benefits verification for ABA therapy isn't one answer.

2
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:12,395
Each case covers eight CPT codes, and each code has its own copay, auth rule, session limit and place of service.

3
00:00:12,615 --> 00:00:15,290
That's sixty-eight scored fields per case.

4
00:00:15,510 --> 00:00:19,660
Grade the whole case right or wrong, and you can't see which field broke.

5
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:26,105
Here, version one gets ninety-seven percent of fields right, but only twenty-two percent of cases.

6
00:00:26,325 --> 00:00:31,675
So this prototype scores every field, and sends only the risky cases to a person.

7
00:00:32,425 --> 00:00:39,350
On the write side, a seeded generator makes two hundred forty synthetic cases from four fictional payers.

8
00:00:39,570 --> 00:00:45,270
An extractor reads each one with two prompt versions, and stores typed results in SQLite.

9
00:00:45,490 --> 00:00:52,715
On the read side, validation, routing and scoring run when a page loads, cached until a gold label changes.

10
00:00:52,935 --> 00:00:57,660
Only the extractor could be a model, through an optional LiteLLM path.

11
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:01,355
Everything else is plain, deterministic Python.

12
00:01:02,105 --> 00:01:03,955
Let's follow one case.

13
00:01:04,175 --> 00:01:11,925
The generator writes a portal-style document with noise, like a copay spelled out in words, and records where each value sits.

14
00:01:12,145 --> 00:01:15,495
Then, extract_mock runs with prompt version two.

15
00:01:15,715 --> 00:01:23,940
It returns a VOBResult, where every field carries a value, a confidence from zero to one, and the source span it came from.

16
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:28,865
Next, validate runs twelve rules against a per-payer table.

17
00:01:29,085 --> 00:01:32,210
Deductible met can't exceed the deductible.

18
00:01:32,430 --> 00:01:35,355
Auth must match the payer's rule for that code.

19
00:01:35,575 --> 00:01:37,350
Then, route decides.

20
00:01:37,570 --> 00:01:44,545
If any field is under the threshold, 0.8 by default, or any rule fails, the case goes to review.

21
00:01:44,765 --> 00:01:46,940
Otherwise, it's auto-approved.

22
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:49,310
Flagged cases sort by risk.

23
00:01:49,530 --> 00:01:58,980
At 0.8, version one auto-approves thirty-two percent of cases, and escaped errors fall from seventy-seven percent of cases to thirteen.

24
00:01:59,730 --> 00:02:05,455
On the case page, source spans glow red for a failed rule, and amber for low confidence.

25
00:02:05,675 --> 00:02:09,025
The reviewer fixes a value, and saves it as gold.

26
00:02:09,245 --> 00:02:15,420
That write is append-only: a new row tagged human, with a timestamp, and the latest row wins.

27
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:21,065
The gold revision changes, so the cached snapshot rebuilds, and accuracy recomputes.

28
00:02:21,815 --> 00:02:29,190
Scoring is exact match after normalization, so twenty-five dollars and twenty-five point zero zero are equal.

29
00:02:29,410 --> 00:02:34,235
For A/B, a seeded hash of the case ID splits cases fifty-fifty.

30
00:02:34,455 --> 00:02:39,480
The bootstrap resamples whole cases, because fields within a case are correlated.

31
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:43,275
Version two fixes session limits and place of service.

32
00:02:43,495 --> 00:02:47,245
But copay drops thirty-nine points for one payer, Northwind.

33
00:02:47,465 --> 00:02:50,415
Averaged across payers, that looks modest.

34
00:02:50,635 --> 00:02:53,360
Any significant regression means hold.

35
00:02:54,110 --> 00:02:56,260
To run it, type make demo.

36
00:02:56,480 --> 00:03:03,655
It generates the data, runs both versions, and serves the UI on port eight thousand, with no API keys.

37
00:03:03,875 --> 00:03:05,600
Now, some gotchas.

38
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:08,270
The mock extractor isn't a model.

39
00:03:08,490 --> 00:03:14,790
It starts from the gold labels and injects seeded errors, so its numbers say nothing about a real system.

40
00:03:15,010 --> 00:03:17,360
Make demo wipes reviewer labels.

41
00:03:17,580 --> 00:03:20,230
Human labels get no second review.

42
00:03:20,450 --> 00:03:25,575
And escaped errors are counted against all cases, not just auto-approved ones.

43
00:03:26,325 --> 00:03:27,800
To recap.

44
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:30,095
Score fields, not cases.

45
00:03:30,315 --> 00:03:32,515
Route on confidence and rules.

46
00:03:32,735 --> 00:03:35,010
Reviewer fixes become gold.

47
00:03:35,230 --> 00:03:39,405
Compare prompts per payer, so regressions can't hide in averages.

48
00:03:39,625 --> 00:03:50,825
Next steps: calibrated thresholds per field, weighting errors by billing impact, double review of human labels, and the same eval on real, de-identified model output.

49
00:03:51,575 --> 00:03:53,300
Thank you for watching.
